#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d18f346-7e92-5bdb-abd0-77ba69d1f332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333820} {:answer/id #uuid "5649faba-09af-5e19-8339-7fa2bd4013e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333821} {:answer/id #uuid "940343d5-6e7e-5084-8b12-1e95728e0b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333822} {:answer/id #uuid "16183522-8bb1-5238-a2cb-bd47c023f219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333823} {:answer/id #uuid "b4664283-1341-582e-94c0-76f79c2c1d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "940343d5-6e7e-5084-8b12-1e95728e0b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333822} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a8336c-8d99-5fd1-b59c-07b5b5e9f477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72e381da-a408-5767-8a79-4cc713ab2c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-20701" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/assets/d1318686a66856057fb04027ac52ff20266e628a498458b283ee93df1e4294eb.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y={e}^{-|x|}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand ${e}^{-|x|}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aed9c37-d74a-5aa1-8fff-8329b2fe065e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333827} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1a2368-159a-50e0-b5a8-74e49c06bdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333828} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ba3430-96d6-54da-8eca-e9f7c54975a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333829} {:answer/id #uuid "50e013d7-fc24-5dee-bd39-d65d624f68e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333830} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f64b8-66eb-55e1-aab0-7f61d89252c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e013d7-fc24-5dee-bd39-d65d624f68e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333830} :answer-field/id #uuid "189df852-c468-5647-8daa-2cc008546104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3151b187-7ba7-51ad-ac17-f4aeb94c10de" :question/math-academy-id "q-20789" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=2+{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{4}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2+0}-\\frac{1}{2+{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea483fb0-880f-56a3-8cea-48a0eb0d4a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333834} {:answer/id #uuid "19af8603-274e-5fc8-80ed-e1dbe0c485fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea483fb0-880f-56a3-8cea-48a0eb0d4a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333834} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d9eebbf-ff83-5c0f-94a9-658a6f43dd41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e60192a-b913-5728-aef5-1ff3e9342621" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b47c68c9-f96e-5f1e-9485-00dfdf1ac9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b47c68c9-f96e-5f1e-9485-00dfdf1ac9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333838} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26f3a22-bca2-51f8-a42d-7a26cc23dffb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3328082-de37-543e-aded-b2717939c17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351605" :question/problem "Find the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ and the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/assets/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{3}))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c8e5dc-8208-54f7-9dbd-a127bded25de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333841} {:answer/id #uuid "b2edc483-9775-564d-824f-42b728521162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333842} {:answer/id #uuid "d5937bbc-90d6-54f3-aec9-c9fcb207806e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333843} {:answer/id #uuid "55c392b9-0605-565d-9646-101ba70e8a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333844} {:answer/id #uuid "b220b1bb-be34-5e7d-a635-4e5564304a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2edc483-9775-564d-824f-42b728521162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333842} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43314a5-2d1d-5846-a608-d79041384b22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00ca25d1-569a-59b9-b8d5-e651fc67373e" :question/math-academy-id "q-5966" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/assets/dd286aa5384dae22d8fff4bb29799078547064926697d71b0b0ca8739641408f.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
